The analyses provided are consistent and accurately identify a classic security vulnerability: SQL Injection. All three analyses correctly identify the improper use of the `java.sql.Statement` API (via string concatenation) and provide the correct remediation using `java.sql.PreparedStatement`. The explanations regarding the separation of code and data, the mechanism of placeholders (`?`), and the specific attack vectors are technically sound.

VERDICT: approved

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class VulnerableExample {
    public void getUserData(String userId) throws SQLException {
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db", "user", "pass");
        Statement statement = conn.createStatement();

        // VULNERABLE: String concatenation allows for SQL Injection.
        // An attacker could provide "1; DROP TABLE users;" as input.
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '" + userId + "'";
        ResultSet rs = statement.executeQuery(query);

        while (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println("User: " + rs.getString("username"));
        }
    }
}
```

***

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class SecureExample {
    public void getUserData(String userId) throws SQLException {
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db", "user", "pass");

        // FIXED: Using PreparedStatement to prevent SQL Injection.
        // The '?' acts as a placeholder, ensuring input is treated only as data.
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?";
        PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement(query);

        // The driver automatically handles escaping of the input.
        pstmt.setString(1, userId);

        ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();

        while (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println("User: " + rs.getString("username"));
        }
    }
}
```

